 And you can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:10.430
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture and place.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.250
 This is Devin Sampson here today with Chelsea Wills.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.490
 And today we're speaking with Farnas Fatemi.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.100
 Farnas is a poet, a writer and a teacher of the craft of writing at UC Santa Cruz.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.100
 And importantly for us, she's a gardener and a lover of tomatoes.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.630
 Her poetry has been published in Ecfrasis, is that how you say it?

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:28.150
 Yeah, okay.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.970
 I read Wheelbarrow and several other poetry journals.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:38.590
 And in the anthologies, Let Me Tell You Where I've Been, and recently, Love and Pomegranates, Artists and Wayfarers on Iran.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.780
 Both compilations of works by Iranian writers outside of Iran.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:49.040
 Her poetry has been nominated for a Pushcart Prize, which honors poetry and literature published by very small presses.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:58.460
 My favorite recent work of hers is in the Tupelo Quarterly, a very personal and lyrical essay about visits to Iran called The Color of the Bricks.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:07.170
 I met Farnas when I took a writing course at UC Santa Cruz in 2002, and then worked as a writing tutor with her students in her class.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:16.020
 Farnas' class focuses on the Middle East, especially Iran and its diaspora, which was an especially charged subject at that time.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.360
 And, well, it certainly, it still is.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:32.470
 She uses a wide range of reading from the political-cultural essays of Edward Said to the graphic novels of Marjane Satrapi and Art Spiegelman to teach writing and intellectual engagement with some of the world's most strained and violent political situations.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:44.540
 Taking her class and later working for her pushed me to find a voice in writing capable of tying together cultural and political aspects of things in a world where the two are very often forced apart.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.090
 Food figures largely in her writings on Iran.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:53.870
 She's also a gardener, and her tomatoes especially are the subject of a vehement love for growing things that's contagious.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.850
 So, Farnas, welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.200
 Thank you.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:03:59.440
 Thanks for having me.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.250
 Let's start by talking about tomatoes.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.810
 When did you start?

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.670
 When did you plant your first serious tomatoes?

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:15.950
 Well, I think the first year I planted was the first year they were serious because it just was like instant love for tomatoes.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:30.180
 But I was actually around a woman who grew tomatoes in the late 90s in Sebastopol, and she had like six raised beds of two or three varieties.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:38.960
 Not a lot, but she had tomatoes all summer, and I lived there, and I took care of them when she was gone, and I was just addicted.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:54.360
 So then when I had a space here to start planting, I tried to plant a few different ones, early 2001, 2002, and the point of shift was 2006.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.120
 I think it was 2006.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:07.320
 When I took a class on starting tomatoes from seed from Cynthia Sandberg at Love Apple Farms, and she was a very small outfit at that point.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:15.040
 She was definitely raising a lot of tomatoes and selling them at a farm stand on the street, right outside her home in Ben Lomond.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:33.770
 But she was just starting to be like a teacher and share the knowledge of starting tomatoes, and she had like 40 different camera film rolls containers of seeds, so that many varieties, and that she'd saved herself.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:45.650
 And she had this morning class, and you got to learn how to just basically put the soil in the cells and spread the seeds out and cover them, all the basics.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:57.040
 But it was, you get to read the descriptions of these 40 different varieties and think, okay, well, what's my house like, or what are my conditions like, but also what color tomato do I want to grow?

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.720
 And I'm like, oh, my God, you get to actually think about what they're going to look like from the very beginning.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.420
 And that was really cool.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:11.050
 So I think we each got to pick like 12 kinds, because that's how many fit in the 144 cell.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:21.550
 And so I picked some that year that I'm still growing, the Jean Flammé and the Black Prince and the Black Cherry.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.520
 And then I don't think, oh, Carmelo, I've grown a lot.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.160
 But I remember just like, I mean, I remember the whole crop.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:38.860
 But so I grew 100, I started 144 seeds, because that's what we just learned how to fill our own tray, and we got to take it home with us.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.180
 And then we never saw her again, really, that was like, go, you know, go propagate.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.900
 And they all, they all came up.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:52.100
 And I'm like, wow, okay, now I have, you know, we knew we'd have to kill some of the babies.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.280
 But I thought, this is amazing.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.080
 I'm going to start, I'm just going to give them to whoever wants them.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.720
 And I started asking around to friends.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.740
 And they're like, oh, okay, yeah, I'll take a couple.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:08.240
 And it was just the coolest feeling to grow that many plants and share them from that moment.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.600
 And then, and have people really excited about it.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:17.140
 And then watch, you know, and then obviously the sort of what happens next is in my own garden, I watched all these things grow.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.200
 And it was, I was hooked.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:26.520
 But part of that first year is the thing that still happens is that I'm like, I just love growing tomatoes and sharing them with people.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.640
 I'm sharing the plants.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:31.720
 So should I go on about what that led to?

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:45.390
 Because so, because I mean, it's really one of the most satisfying things about growing anything at this point is the fact that every February, I start, I start like 200 seeds.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:47.700
 I start, it's 20 different varieties.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:59.720
 I now have in my front row, which I didn't show you, three tiers of lights, of grow lights that they get to live under.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.840
 And it's like, you know, from a gardening with kids, like educational catalog, I saw it.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:09.440
 And I'm like, I'm going to save up for that thing because it's just so, it's made everything so easy.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:18.990
 But I used to have in the front windows, the south facing windows, just like just tables of these seeds coming, starting through February and March.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:24.730
 And, and then I'd transplant them up, you know, to the four inch pots.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:28.580
 And then I'd, as soon as I could find them homes, I'd get them out into the world.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.040
 And someone said that first year, they're like, oh, you're like, you know, the Johnny Appleseed of tomatoes.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.390
 And I'm like, yeah, I guess I, I, I just, I'm not like on a mission or anything.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:42.980
 I just, it's so great to grow these and then, and then have, be able to share them.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:48.240
 And there's, and, and that happens with like the fruit that comes off my trees and, you know, the lemons.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.940
 If I have abundance, it's a, such a, it's such a great feeling.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:53.560
 And I'm sure that's, I'm not alone in that.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:09:03.740
 Just it's, it's so satisfying, but the, the having little baby plants and, um, and a community of people all over that wants them.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:09.640
 So even that first year, some of them went to my mom in LA and she had a patio.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:14.290
 She didn't have any place to plant, but she and her boyfriend planted a few pots.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.610
 And I thought, it's so cool that things I started here are in LA.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:29.570
 And then the, and then from the second year on, they've gone to, um, really good friends in Nevada city, um, to see bright and to the mountains and to, you know, the, the, the neighborhood, um, here.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:34.320
 And then, you know, it's just, and then I sent seeds the following year to England.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.280
 Cause I have a former student who now lives in England.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:43.400
 And then this last year I sent seeds to Jamaica cause I have really good friends with a kid who's an 11 year old who wanted to learn about gardening.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.480
 I'm like, you're getting some of my seeds, you know?

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:55.050
 So, so I saved them every year and I since decided to start saving the ones that I love, um, mostly just out of like, I need to make sure I have these.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:08.000
 Um, and then it's, that's another thing I talk about, but it's turned into more about the saving, but, um, but it, it's just been like, so to have like an arm load of plants go out with a friend.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.540
 And that, that first year that I invited my writing program friends to come over, I took pictures of all of them cause they had their like, like their dozen plants and they're walking out.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:18.240
 And I'm like, this is, it's just, it's so satisfying.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.700
 It was, it was really, really fun.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:26.340
 What reports do you get back from those plants at this time of year?

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:35.010
 Well, this time of year, I mean, people are always asking like, oh, um, you know, there, some of them are like, do you still have tomatoes?

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.510
 Cause mine are starting to slow down.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:38.030
 Am I doing something wrong?

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:40.650
 And I'm like, you're not, you know, no one's doing anything wrong.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:46.530
 Um, and, but a lot of times it's, I mean, right now it's just, people are so happy.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:51.410
 People are so, I mean, the Jean Flammé is, it's a medium sized, do you know this, this tomato?

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:52.910
 It's a medium sized orange tomato.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:11:02.460
 And it's, it's my, it's in the running for my favorite, but I can, I can never give up that spot entirely to the, the Black Prince is really great too.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:08.720
 And so it was chocolate stripes, but it's, it grows sometimes until November and it's still tasty.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:19.110
 It's this one here, um, this, this orange one, um, it, um, people, it like the last couple of weeks are like, I'm still having Jean Flammé.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:19.970
 It's so good.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:21.790
 I'm so glad.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:27.760
 Um, people will, will call and say like, oh, you know, Black Crème didn't do well.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.880
 Well, Black Crème is a Siberian tomato, right?

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:38.390
 So the fact it's usually does really well in Santa Cruz, but in the drought conditions and then the heat of this year, it's, it's been very sad.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.870
 And so, and mine were tasty, but they, the plant died.

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:47.410
 Like, I'm really sorry I planted you in the full sun exposure, a hundred degree weather in July.

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:51.610
 And it's kind of exceptional, but, um, so I love that.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:57.650
 I get, I, I ask people to let me know what they're, how they're doing, but I don't force anyone to.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.060
 And, but I always get, I get like emails and I get Facebook posts and I get from England.

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:05.540
 I've gotten pictures, you know, posted of here's the babies here.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:06.500
 Here's the grand babies.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.140
 And it's, it's great.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:10.520
 Yeah.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.110
 When did you start saving seeds?

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:20.640
 So I, so I was really inspired by Cynthia and I started saving that first year and I saved, um,

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:28.050
 she didn't, she didn't, um, talk about, she didn't talk about how to save, but I just started researching that.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:32.080
 And, um, I started saving a few varieties that year.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.760
 And like I said, I think I've, I've actually had, I had blight.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:43.130
 So I, at one point I, um, got rid of all the seeds cause I didn't know if that would, what would be affected.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:13:05.380
 And I started over with Jean Plamets, but otherwise it's been like contiguous, you know, from that, so 2009 to now, those Jean Plamets from a seed I got from someplace, um, I can't remember the name now in Carmel, um, Tomato Fest, Gary, Gary, uh, Gary, somebody is, he's been saving seeds.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.870
 He's been creating his own seed, um, collection.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.760
 And so I got them, I got a lot of them from him that year.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:31.290
 Um, but so I started saving in that first year and then started figuring out, okay, this plant did really well here and it's also really tasty and it's also really beautiful next to the orange and the black prints and the, um, the other, the, the variety I think is what I was after.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:43.520
 So I started trying different things for the first few years, but I, but as soon as I found something that I knew I wanted to keep, I start, I would save it and, um, keep growing from my own saved collection.

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:56.260
 And some people get seeds from me and I'm totally, I'd share as much as I, as anybody wants to, but a lot of people just want to, um, they want, they're like, they're happy to just get the plant and go from there, which is fine.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:14:05.730
 Um, but now I have a few people locally that other gardener and farmer friends that, um, are just also getting excited about seed saving.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:17.010
 And so this year we had two different swaps, you know, just, you know, fun, informal things, but it's really great because it's nice to, um, it's nice not to ever buy seeds again.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:29.860
 It's kind of my idea, but, um, and I'm sure part of why I was attracted to it was it's very, it's thrifty and, um, it feels like the right way to, to spend that resources.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:38.570
 Um, and, but it's also just like, you see, you get to, somebody else saves the kind of lettuce that you would never try to try something new.

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:42.160
 And so the first few years of seed saving was like for tip with tomatoes.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:47.500
 I was really just trying, like probably, I probably tried 40 different varieties because I wanted to just experiment.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:57.400
 But now I'm like so addicted to the ones that I love that I think Paul and I have chosen like about a dozen that we're just always going to have if we, if they're viable.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:05.850
 Um, and then I grow a few more than that because other people like things that I don't like, you know, want my friends to have what they want.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:08.710
 So how do you decide what goes next to each other?

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.780
 Um, in the garden or in the bowl?

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:22.740
 Oh, well, you mean, cause I said that about, yeah, it's, it's, um, it's all, it's all about the visual composition and it's just imagining it.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:29.980
 But I love, you know, I love the juxtaposition of a big tomato with a small tomato and a light yellow with a deep red.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:35.450
 And, um, I grew up around, uh, um, visual beauty.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:47.400
 My mom's a designer and she's, so she, we, we lived, we lived appreciating aesthetics and, um, and I am not very talented as a visual artist, but, um, but I like it.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:56.640
 And so I think that, that that's totally part of the, the appeal is, is how's it going to look just sitting on the counter and it's not fetishized.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:06.640
 It's, it's, it's really like, I want, I'm going to appreciate my tomatoes between the point when I harvest them and when I eat them and when I cut them up for the salad and then serve them.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:19.520
 And so often, you know, I just, like I have today, I just cut them up and it's just tomatoes because of course we should eat them with the cheese, but they look so nice as just a bowl of cut tomatoes and they'll taste really good together.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:22.380
 But why not just appreciate them like that?

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.700
 So it's, it's the, that variety, it's that, the palette.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:33.390
 I did try growing the, um, supposedly black tomato, which is the indigo rose, dark purple tomato.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.190
 And it, and I, it was great.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:36.550
 It was, I mean, beautiful.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:40.010
 The plant looks great, but it just wasn't that tasty.

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:49.230
 And so I, I did it that one year and we appreciated it and it, it really added this like spec, end of the spectrum, but I didn't grow it again.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:50.150
 That was two years ago.

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:58.430
 Um, and then the, the light colored ones that are considered white tomatoes are really, really pale.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.810
 And I haven't found one yet that's that tasty.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.390
 So it also keeps getting the ax this year.

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:09.810
 I just, the lightest I grew was the, um, well, yeah, it was Chuck's yellow.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:17.110
 Chelsea and I spend a lot of time talking about, um, or doing research about biodiversity and people's gardens.

00:17:17.350 --> 00:17:24.660
 And so it strikes me that the tomato growers are some of the most vehement lovers of diversity.

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:28.990
 And they have creative names to tomatoes.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:33.450
 There's, there's, um, the diverse, the people plant dozens of varieties.

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:38.880
 So why do you, what is it about tomatoes that makes that, that diversity attractive?

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:40.800
 Gosh, that's a good question.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:46.410
 Um, well, they are when, when they're good, they're so different.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:56.210
 They're really, I, I, and I, and that's been part of the pleasure is having people discover how much flavor tomatoes can have and how nuanced.

00:17:56.290 --> 00:18:04.630
 They can be, um, and, and, you know, there are a few different kinds of red tomatoes in this bowl right now there and they taste different.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:09.570
 And to, to be, to learn how to appreciate that is, is really fun.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:15.090
 But it's also, it's, um, it's, um, it's also very particular to individuals.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:23.230
 You know, you'd have tomato, people love certain kinds of tomatoes and they, they've learned that they really don't like a black tomato for whatever reason.

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:30.750
 But, um, but I think they're different, even black tomatoes all taste different, you know, or brown, I guess it's the, the purple.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:33.880
 Um, they all taste, they taste different from each other.

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:40.990
 So you, it's really fun to, to, um, it's just, it's joyful, that kind of diversity.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:48.940
 Of course, I've learned a lot about, um, branching out because I, I first only grew tomatoes and thought, oh, this is just it.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:58.040
 And then partly I realized I couldn't do that to my soil and partly I realized that there's a lot of things that would grow well here and that I wanted, that I would enjoy.

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:02.300
 So cucumbers and peppers are the big ones that like how satisfying to be able to grow those.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:10.320
 Um, but, and so a wider diversity of edible plants has also been, you know, pretty interesting.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:24.580
 But the thing is that there's so many varieties of tomatoes that do, um, they, that need to be preserved, you know, need to be saved and need to be, to be appreciated, I guess.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:32.880
 And so growing the diversity ensures that, I mean, I know I'm just doing, I'm like one person, but I'm caring for the seeds.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:34.980
 They're, they're, they're, they're protected.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:39.760
 You know, if something happens to me, they're there, you know, and, and I've done my part.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:46.170
 Now, I didn't tell you too, um, one point this was, I, I, God, I have it written down.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:47.730
 It's probably five years ago now.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:20:00.240
 Uh, John, John Fulme and an Isis candy cherry were too close and they got cross pollinated and, uh, a hybrid showed up that I didn't realize what it was first.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.500
 And I was like, what is this tomato?

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:02.780
 I didn't plant this.

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:06.150
 Um, it's an, it's, what's it doing here?

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.650
 And so I kind of backtracked to figure out what had happened.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:14.030
 And, and, um, and I made a note like this, it was tasty.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:21.620
 It was really, it was like a slightly large cherry, um, red and, um, really good raw and really good in salads.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:24.740
 And so I just made a bunch of notes like, okay, this is what happened.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.880
 Save these seeds, um, see if it stabilizes.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.470
 And so it did.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:31.830
 The next year it came back exactly the same.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:35.090
 And so it's been like, it's been four or five seasons of this.

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:36.170
 So now I've named it.

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:37.190
 It's Escalona red.

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:41.770
 Cause it happened right here on Escalona drive and my neighbors love it.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:44.810
 I mean, they, it's the, we know it will grow well here.

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:48.450
 We know in this particular microclimate and it's, it is tasty.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:52.210
 And I have actually a one set pair of friends that just moved to Watsonville.

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:56.290
 And now that's all they, this year they got six Escalona reds for me and nothing else.

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.250
 So they don't care about diversity.

00:20:58.370 --> 00:20:59.870
 They're like, I found the tomato I love.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:01.210
 But I think that's part of it.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:04.570
 You can't find the tomato you love unless you try a bunch of different things.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.820
 I'm curious, did other things in your garden,

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:13.600
 did you start planting other things in your garden based on what would be good with tomatoes?

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.660
 Well, um, partly the cucumbers.

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:17.720
 Absolutely.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:23.560
 Um, because there's, it is partly it's a, um, it's tomatoes and cucumbers.

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:27.300
 Everyone loves that, but it's a very Persian thing to put those,

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:29.860
 that and lemon and some red onion.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:34.470
 It's a, that's a Shirazi salad and it's, and it's a little mint.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:37.870
 Um, that's, that's a really good combination.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:41.490
 Although I keep reading other people have versions of that salad,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:42.890
 but I, I think of it as Persian.

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:48.600
 Um, and, and that, I, that was definitely like, if I could,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.040
 I have mint and now I have a lemon tree.

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:55.890
 So like, um, and I grew, and this year I grew successful batch of red onions.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.070
 So now I have everything in the garden for that salad.

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:00.190
 I'm like, ah, it's so great.

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:05.590
 Yeah. So, I mean, partly it's what would go well and partly like a specific dishes.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:12.680
 So then I had padrones when they, um, meter street farms of the first farmer here that grew

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.140
 padrones and sold them at the market.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:18.680
 And Paul and I had them and that summer we had them and we're like, these are just so fun.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:22.680
 It was a long time ago now, but it was, it, we both remember that happening.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.840
 And I said, I'm going to try to, I'm going to try to grow those.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:30.490
 Cause that was just, we would, we would buy them even though it was a, an indulgence.

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:35.330
 And so I started growing padrones and I thought, okay, what other kinds of peppers would I,

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:37.610
 could I grow that might work here?

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:42.390
 It's not hot enough for sweet peppers, but the padrones and the serranos and the jalapenos

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.450
 and then now Peruvian, um, they all grow well.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:50.470
 And then just turns out that range that I just described is kind of a variety of colors.

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:56.250
 And so it's really satisfying to have that like palette of, look, look at these peppers,

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:59.390
 you know, just to go with all the, the tomatoes.

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:05.630
 Cucumbers, I'm pretty, I'm pretty just stuck on like a nice small Persian size, you know,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:07.590
 what they call Persian size green cucumber.

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:09.910
 I am now growing the long Armenians too.

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:14.270
 And then again, those are, it's neat cause they're different, but, um, but there's nothing

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:15.210
 that beats those.

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.470
 So I don't know.

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:19.470
 Did I, did I address what you were asking?

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:20.270
 I kind of went off.

00:23:20.770 --> 00:23:21.170
 Okay.

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:28.670
 I have this feeling that I'm just like, well, I have a question that I'm not quite sure how

00:23:28.670 --> 00:23:33.770
 to ask, but cause it seems so obvious, but it's, um, it just seems obvious to me that

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:41.520
 someone who is a writer loves gardening because there's this, there's this act of this tension

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:44.340
 between inventing and reinventing the world all the time.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:49.620
 And also just noticing what's there that are both are part of both of those crafts.

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:51.320
 You're so smart.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:53.780
 Yeah.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:55.780
 I guess that's true.

00:23:55.960 --> 00:24:02.090
 The, so part of what I think for me, and I don't know, because I don't know, I don't

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:06.610
 know a lot of people that do both that way, but I know that what gardening has taught me

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:11.570
 about writing is part of what you're talking about is that you're inventing and you're reinventing

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:20.090
 and you're, you're making something new all the time and it's not new at all because you

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:23.110
 grew it before or it already existed in the world once.

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:29.590
 And, but there's, but it's incredibly generative to start something from seed and watch it take

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:29.910
 off.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:41.030
 Um, so in that way, it's, um, it's, it's like a lesson in that act of, of, of, of just

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:47.630
 of, of making something and not, I guess, not thinking you have to be one original.

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:54.440
 Like there's, there's such a, when I first started thinking of myself as a poet, I, I was under,

00:24:54.440 --> 00:25:02.220
 I had that burden of like, oh, I have to be really, really unique and really, you know,

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:02.880
 really original.

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.620
 And that's, that can, that's like paralyzing.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:06.000
 Right.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:09.760
 So, and then you don't really think, listen to your voice if you're thinking about that.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:19.270
 And so, so to gardening kind of keeps teaching me that lesson over and over that, that it feels

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:29.360
 really good to, to make things and that, um, it's not, it's more than anything, that process

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:33.780
 of, of connecting to that pleasure is the important thing.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:39.700
 And then wonderful things can come out of that, but, and might, and they could be unique and

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:41.830
 they could be important for other people.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:46.670
 But I think starting with that, that sort of connection is, is key.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:52.620
 And the thing is also in gardening, and I don't know if this is where you were going with it.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:57.530
 Um, a lot of things fail, a lot of things die, right?

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:00.090
 And you have failures of seasons.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:05.390
 And then that blight season, I, my best friend died that same year and I, and suddenly, and,

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:10.210
 and then I had blight and I'm like, this is so not fair.

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:15.550
 Um, and, and I'm, you know, and I was like, should I not grow, you know, maybe next year

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.430
 I won't grow anything because I don't want to associate it with that feeling.

00:26:18.430 --> 00:26:25.890
 And it was really, it was intensely emotional, but at the same time, like I want the next

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:30.340
 year, I had the impulse to plant, you know, and I like, so I did.

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:38.930
 And, and to, to sort of not think, well, I'm in my, and to not have the, the, the knowledge

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.770
 that it could fail, stop me from doing it is, that's huge.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:48.420
 Um, and I, you know, again, I think sometimes that seems really obvious, but it's, it's

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:56.970
 also, it's something I feel like I keep like relearning, you know, like, like, duh, there

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:57.670
 it comes again.

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:04.190
 Um, so, so what the thing is like, I know that I know, I do know a lot of writers that also

00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:06.090
 garden, but I know a lot that don't.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:13.240
 And I know just for me, it's a, it is a really important place of, of, uh, where I, I, partly

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:20.940
 it's, I, I can get up from the writing and go be in a different space and sort of, and

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:26.180
 have that be okay and have it be satisfying and not think I'm running away from something.

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:32.980
 Um, but sometimes when I'm not writing or I'm not, I'm not, I'm not inspired or, you

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:37.200
 know, but that's a, but not quite the right word, but I'm not connected to writing or I'm

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:42.320
 not, I'm feeling bad about myself as a writer or like, I'll never write again, which happens,

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:43.860
 you know, every year for like months.

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:49.520
 Um, at least if I'm in the, in the garden or, or, or doing something with food, I mean,

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:54.330
 in the kitchen too, um, I, I feel connected to that part of me.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:58.950
 That's, that has, that's that, that creative impulse or then the impulse to, to make and

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.190
 to make something beautiful.

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:05.070
 Oh, there's so many good things in that.

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:07.310
 So hard to pick which direction to go.

00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:13.290
 It makes me think about another, one of your passions is travel, right?

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:17.380
 So when I hear you talk about that, I, I think about it as a studio practice, right?

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:21.940
 Creating this space for experimentation over and over and over again, right?

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:23.980
 And the garden is that space.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:27.040
 One of those spaces that you come back to, but for you, travel is also.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:27.600
 Yes.

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:28.640
 One of those spaces.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:34.930
 And so can you talk about all those things together in terms of how food plays a role

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:35.730
 in that?

00:28:35.990 --> 00:28:36.830
 Oh yeah.

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:43.450
 Well, it's true that, um, well, I, I think there's a connection to the, the travel impulse.

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:47.410
 What you're, what you were saying, you, I haven't articulated it that way, but I think that

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:53.410
 one thing about travel is that you, you don't know what's going to happen.

00:28:54.720 --> 00:29:00.350
 And you, you have, in order to, to have a satisfying travel experience, you have to be okay with

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:01.530
 that kind of all the time.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:09.030
 Um, and like I was saying, I think growing food, you have that, you're doing the same

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.350
 thing pretty much all the time.

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:14.730
 You, you, I mean, I count on certain things at this point with tomatoes, but there's still

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:20.350
 like this year in May, the, it was really windy and dry here.

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:22.950
 And I had planted, the flowers had already started.

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:28.770
 And I think I lost a lot of tomato blossoms that's never happened before in the 10 years

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:30.630
 that I've grown in this, in this front yard.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:37.640
 So like, you don't know, but, um, but I think the sort of what you're, the brain does when

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:45.270
 in, in, um, growing is the same, is like the same thing that it does for me and cooking

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:56.400
 and sort of, um, approaching, um, eating, um, that it's, it's, I'm a, I'm a pretty adventurous

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:59.960
 eater and I'm a pretty adventurous, reasonably adventurous chef.

00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:03.840
 I do like recipes, but I often tend to like just start there and go.

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:14.630
 Um, and, and Paul is too, we both are pretty, we've cultivated like a space of our kitchen

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:21.670
 and our, our, this little, this area as like, as well, a lot of pleasure and, um, a place

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:25.880
 to, um, a place to see what happens.

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:31.360
 He is always hearing like some new dish that he wants to try, you know, and it's great.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:37.240
 And I think, and he's a writer also, um, I think there's part of that, that just desire

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:42.790
 to continue to grow, um, and to continue to, to like the world gets bigger, but it's also,

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:45.470
 it's getting bigger just right in here and I'm not home.

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:47.650
 So there's, there, there's that.

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:54.050
 Um, but, and, but we also really encourage each other on the travel side because we are

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:59.490
 both, um, it's easy for us to get into travel mode.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:05.720
 We travel really well together, so that helps, but I think it's, we're both also just cultivating

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.770
 that, um, explorer mentality.

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:12.170
 I'm like, what will I learn from this experience?

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:17.290
 And we've had some bad experiences, but we've still, you know, we still keep going out and

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:18.310
 doing it.

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:23.200
 Um, when I hear you talk about all those things, I feel like you could be talking about writing

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:25.980
 or you could be talking about a visual arts practice.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:31.930
 In my mind, they all really mimic each other, those processes.

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:32.730
 Right.

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:35.940
 So, yeah, I didn't, I didn't realize that about travel.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:41.200
 I just knew that like I've been in somebody who likes to travel since I was 19 years old

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:48.690
 and I kind of didn't, I didn't, I guess I didn't trust it as like, as nurturing.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:51.270
 I just thought it was like, I wanted an adventure.

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:55.250
 I'm going to save up and have it, you know, but I didn't think of it that, that way.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:59.930
 Um, it's in for a long time.

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:05.730
 And I kind of thought, I kind of thought of myself as like this, you know, travel addict

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:11.330
 or because, and partly because I had people in my life saying, why do you travel so much?

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:15.430
 And I thought of it as judgmental, but I, and it might probably wasn't, it was probably

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:21.790
 just jealous, but, um, but I didn't, I, for a long time, it didn't seem like it's a sustenance

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:22.470
 in that way.

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:30.080
 And then, um, I think, you know, Paul and I started traveling together and actually have

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:35.360
 that being validated by, you know, somebody else, a partner that's as excited about it

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:39.260
 and, um, made me think of like, what is it that we get at, you know, maybe just ask the

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:40.780
 questions, what am I getting out of travel?

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:45.000
 That's, and you're right, it's, it's very similar, but for a long time, it was this other

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:45.340
 thing.

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:49.820
 It was like that, like kind of secret, secret pleasure that was like, oh, I shouldn't do

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:52.920
 that so much, but why not?

00:32:54.330 --> 00:33:00.520
 Um, so yeah, I mean, the questions now are about like carbon, you know, and how much travel

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:03.260
 do I really want to do now that I've done so much in my life?

00:33:03.260 --> 00:33:06.980
 Do I really need to go away for the same kind of experience?

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:11.150
 And I don't, I think you're, you, what you're saying is right.

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:17.330
 That there's a lot of places here close, close by that the same kind of like this, a practice

00:33:17.330 --> 00:33:18.590
 can, can get you.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:24.720
 Um, and that's what I'm starting to appreciate in my, in my like, you know, elder years, in

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:25.480
 my forties.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:29.160
 Um, I, I sort of think more about that than I used to.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:31.630
 I have a question.

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:33.070
 Can I ask you?

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:41.060
 Well, it makes me think about your relationship with Iran and how is that travel?

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.400
 Is that like a return to home?

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:47.060
 I mean, you, you live here, you were born here, you're Iranian American.

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:53.970
 So you are definitely from this place, but in your writing, what was that quote that you

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:54.650
 wrote?

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:59.030
 I'm not going to be able to find it now about returning to somewhere that you're not from,

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:01.230
 but that you have always been from.

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:02.810
 Or since I'm paraphrasing.

00:34:02.810 --> 00:34:03.750
 That was a good paraphrase.

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:04.030
 Okay.

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:05.630
 Um, yeah.

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:10.110
 So it's, so it's partly traveled to Iran and it's partly traveled to a culture, you know,

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:19.580
 to, to, uh, uh, a sort of spirit of, of my family and, um, and where, where I come from.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:30.130
 Um, so yes, um, I still have the desire to go, go there, but I also, you know, use writing

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:36.620
 and I use food and I, and to, to be in that space of, of the, the, that culture.

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:43.870
 Um, I grew up around a lot of Iranians and in, um, from the time I was probably seven or

00:34:43.870 --> 00:34:47.930
 eight, cause that's when the revolution happened and more, more of our family came to Southern

00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:48.350
 California.

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:55.250
 So I grew up feeling at home in a, in a kind of space that wasn't, you know, anywhere else.

00:34:55.730 --> 00:35:03.250
 Um, and, but it was also this interesting mix of language and, and Iranians and Americans

00:35:03.250 --> 00:35:04.650
 and traditions.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:12.470
 Um, and when I got to be like a teenager, I wasn't that, you know, proud of it.

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:17.560
 And, and also, you know, I grew, I was a preteen and teen during the revolution.

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:24.890
 So I was, I experienced a lot of, of hatred and just, you know, racism, the kind of thing

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:27.990
 that you're like, you don't realize what's going on when you're that age, but you've

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:30.100
 in retrospect, I figured out, Oh, okay.

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:32.400
 That's, that's why I felt the way I did.

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:35.660
 I stopped calling myself Iranian and started calling myself Persian.

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:38.100
 You know, those, a lot of things that other people experienced.

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:41.780
 I didn't realize other people were experiencing them, but, but now I know.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:49.990
 And so, um, as a, as an adult, I started feeling like I had moved so far away from my,

00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:52.890
 my, um, origins and something.

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:58.330
 I mean, those are really my origins are Iranian American or American Iranian, um, that I,

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:04.500
 I, that, that it, it seemed important to me to try to figure out what part of what, what

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:06.280
 made, what's made me who I am.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:09.320
 And then I, a lot of what's made me who I am.

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:14.530
 I really love, you know, I really, like, I really care about the certain things about

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:15.470
 Iranian culture.

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:20.770
 And there's, I can be critical of certain things too, but I think there's a, there's so much

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:29.320
 that we don't have access to as West in, in America about Iran that, um, it's, it's really,

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:31.570
 it's, I'm curious about.

00:36:31.570 --> 00:36:35.210
 And so it's partly a personal like attempt to understand.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:41.990
 And partly I feel like I have an act, I have access as an, as a writer, I feel like I do

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:52.040
 want to, to, um, articulate those things that I see because I'm, I'm the person that can't,

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:54.500
 you know, I'm the, I don't, it's not a burden.

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:56.800
 And it's not like I'm like sort of on a mission.

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:01.610
 It's just, you know, you see things that you've experienced things you're noticing.

00:37:01.610 --> 00:37:07.040
 And as you return to it and I sort of go back, keep going back to this material, um, I want

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:10.800
 to save them and I want to put them, have them be real in the world.

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:19.180
 So, um, so that's, I think part of the answer to your question.

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:23.710
 And, um, and, and I haven't been able to go since, well, it's been almost 10 years.

00:37:23.710 --> 00:37:28.520
 And, um, but I, when I did travel there, I traveled regularly and then got to live there

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:29.100
 for a while.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:33.870
 And it was just a great, it was a, it was a great experience, a surprisingly good experience

00:37:33.870 --> 00:37:38.210
 right after the first time I went back as an adult was three months before 9-11.

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:39.830
 And then I went back right after that.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:50.450
 And that's when I think you and I met, um, so it, yeah, it's an opportunity and, um, something

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:53.530
 I, something I feel like is important to stay connected to.

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:54.720
 Any questions?

00:37:55.720 --> 00:38:01.030
 Well, so something that stands out to me in your writing about Iran is you write about

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:02.050
 memory and food.

00:38:02.530 --> 00:38:10.870
 And, um, I think that's a deeply feminist act, first of all, because I think it continues

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:13.300
 to be how our bodies tell stories, right?

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:15.940
 It's eating together and sharing those moments.

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:22.020
 But also it makes me think about these tastes, like these memories of tastes that you don't

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:23.260
 even know that you have.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:31.490
 So can you talk about, can you describe some experiences like that in Iran of maybe a homecoming

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:33.030
 or something through these tastes?

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:33.390
 Yeah.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:40.700
 Well, so I think I, um, in Love and Pomegranates, which is that one of the anthologies you mentioned,

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:43.080
 uh, the piece in there is called Shirazi Limes.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:49.840
 And part of what I was trying to do with that piece was capture that feeling of the way something

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:55.170
 tastes that you might have, you might have grown up eating.

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.400
 Um, and it just ha, it just tastes totally different in a different place.

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:06.580
 Um, and so I even, you know, I even had those kinds of limes, um, in the States because some,

00:39:06.740 --> 00:39:10.920
 you know, you can get them and, um, and there's Mexican limes are similar.

00:39:11.550 --> 00:39:21.430
 Um, but when I, um, when I went to visit in 2001, I went back to this space that was my

00:39:21.430 --> 00:39:25.740
 grandmother's apartment, her living room in her apartment that I'd been in when I was five

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:30.340
 and when I was seven and that was the last time and she, this first trip back, I went

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:35.230
 with her and my mom and my twin sister and I, I spearheaded that trip.

00:39:35.350 --> 00:39:35.970
 I really wanted to go.

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:37.330
 And I said, shouldn't we all go together?

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.590
 And everyone said, yes, my, it was my grandmother's last time back.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:51.520
 Um, and, and, um, we were sitting in the bunch of people, um, sitting around in that living

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:55.880
 room and I, there were, you know, I don't know why there were limes, but I was like, I'm

00:39:55.880 --> 00:39:57.720
 going to cut one and, you know, eat it.

00:39:57.720 --> 00:40:00.240
 And I, I eat it like some Persians do.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:03.940
 I like squeeze the juice into my mouth and then gum it and teeth it.

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:05.800
 And then you turn it inside out and eat the whole pulp.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:10.040
 And I was just there, um, doing that.

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:13.800
 And my uncle was like, oh, these great, you know, my, and, and I said, yeah, you know,

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:15.000
 this is it.

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:15.980
 And they taste amazing.

00:40:16.040 --> 00:40:19.140
 They've always tasted amazing, but they taste a certain way here.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:20.300
 You know, they just have that.

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:23.800
 It's like, yes, you're never going to taste this like this in the States.

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:25.600
 And I was thinking, yeah, that's so funny.

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:29.300
 We get so proud about food, but at the same time he was totally right.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:31.320
 So that, that's what I was trying to write about.

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:37.610
 But at the same time, like, um, so that happened and, oh, and then what I remembered in that

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:41.390
 scene, I was 33 years old.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:49.410
 Um, I remember, um, doing that in that same living room and my aunt, my mom's older sister

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:52.150
 turned to me and say, don't start doing that.

00:40:52.210 --> 00:40:52.510
 I did.

00:40:52.610 --> 00:40:54.930
 I've done that all my life and it's just going to ruin your teeth.

00:40:54.970 --> 00:41:00.510
 But she's like smiling while she says she knows like, and she just, you're going to want to do it.

00:41:00.890 --> 00:41:03.380
 So, and I, but I was like, oh my God, I remember that.

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:05.780
 I remember Friday, like I was the first person to say that.

00:41:06.260 --> 00:41:10.060
 Um, so there's a court, you know, food and family and love.

00:41:10.080 --> 00:41:12.160
 It's all, it's all like packed in there.

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:16.220
 And, and Iranians eat together like in great groups.

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:21.190
 And they, it's a really, it's growing up in LA, it was a really, like the one thing we

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:23.650
 would get together on the weekend and have dinner at somebody's house.

00:41:23.650 --> 00:41:27.030
 And it was always just a totally satisfying, like fun.

00:41:27.190 --> 00:41:31.710
 I'm, I like, I'm comfortable in large groups of people because of that, you know, like that

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:36.540
 size, you know, not like, I don't like to go to concerts anymore, but, um, but I, it's

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:41.340
 that I'm like, there's just a level of community and love and, and festivity.

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:48.850
 Um, so that, that happens to me more too, when I'm in eating in Iran is that like, people

00:41:48.850 --> 00:41:54.310
 are so, you know, like, isn't this food great, isn't this yummy, you know, past that, past

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:54.570
 this.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:59.550
 And there's a kind of like, there's just a verbal enthusiasm that I'm sure people in other

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:04.620
 cultures have the enthusiasm and some of them are like that, but Iranians are definitely

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.780
 like, they savor it.

00:42:05.780 --> 00:42:10.380
 And so eat, just eating with other people in Iran is a, is a kind of experience.

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:17.150
 Um, I, in that piece, the color of the bricks, I also wrote about this, um, Augustary, which

00:42:17.150 --> 00:42:20.940
 is August is, it means like meat water.

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:22.560
 It sounds like it's a stew.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:29.900
 It's basically just kind of traditional, um, all classes have, you know, it's a very working

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:30.940
 work a day meal.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:36.040
 You can go get it in the middle of the day and love, you know, been just cooking it and

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:39.580
 it's tasty and good for you and reasonably easy to do.

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:43.490
 Um, sometimes there's no meat, it's just the, you know, the meat, the broth.

00:42:43.810 --> 00:42:50.170
 But, um, the, we, my uncle, the last time I was there took me to this, in Esfahan, to

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:50.870
 this Augustary.

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:52.430
 And he's like, Oh, you'll love this.

00:42:52.510 --> 00:42:52.770
 I know.

00:42:52.850 --> 00:43:00.390
 You know, and what was great is that it's like a rundown kind of greasy and filthy and tiny

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:02.070
 upstairs place, hot.

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:03.520
 And he knew I'd love it.

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:09.270
 Like I was just, I was so glad that he, he, he learned who I was, that he wasn't ashamed.

00:43:09.430 --> 00:43:15.270
 He wasn't trying, he, he, he knows some fancy places and he's very, you know, very, very good

00:43:15.270 --> 00:43:18.850
 palate and can eat well, but he like, you have to try this place.

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:25.070
 And so part of the experience is just like that, you know, every, mostly men were there

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:27.160
 having their midday meal.

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:33.290
 Um, but I was with my younger, young cousin, she's very young at the time.

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:40.360
 And, um, and her mom and him, and we just, we had this, like we had this lunch and it

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:41.920
 just, and it tasted so good.

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:44.240
 And I've had that, I've had abgushed all my life.

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:49.520
 And it has, first of all, meats taste different in different places because of what the animal's

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:49.780
 eating.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:50.040
 Right.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:55.500
 So there's that, but, um, but I, and I don't, you know, I don't usually eat much meat, but

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:58.890
 in Iran, I'm always like, I'm gonna, I'm totally gonna try that.

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:00.410
 I, that's, I've got to.

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:05.960
 And, um, and it just had, it was like, it, it is the, the Proustian thing about like, it

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:10.360
 brings all like the memories back of where you first ate it or where you only ate it.

00:44:10.360 --> 00:44:14.790
 But it's also just that all the meal, all the abgushed I've ever had were there in that

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:15.870
 abgustery with me.

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:26.190
 And then this, this, this one that was my, was my uncle's favorite and, um, special because

00:44:26.190 --> 00:44:26.930
 of that too.

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:39.640
 There's that tension in that, that piece specifically about the Shirazi Lime of, um, where being in

00:44:39.640 --> 00:44:41.420
 a, in a home talking about food.

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:45.200
 And I think this comes a lot, a lot of moments in your, your writing.

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:53.610
 There's, um, there's this, this recognition of like a deep creativity, um, in feminine spaces

00:44:53.610 --> 00:44:59.400
 and not necessarily you're in Iran and they're not necessarily voluntarily feminine spaces,

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:05.460
 but there's this feeling of deep feminism and talking about what goes on in a kitchen and

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:06.580
 what goes on in a garden.

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:11.200
 Cause it has to, I mean, cause it's not going to go away.

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:20.880
 I mean, um, the impulse to, to, I mean, part of it is the impulse to make, make great feeling

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:21.880
 spaces, right.

00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:25.480
 That's not going to, you can't, you can't stop that.

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:32.790
 So it is, it's, um, for sure for a lot of women in Iran, there's an act of like, um, of

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:38.160
 rebellion or of just empowerment to say, I'm going to have to cover my windows and I have

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:41.380
 to, you know, not, you can't see me dance, but I'm still going to have people over and

00:45:41.380 --> 00:45:45.300
 I'm going to, going to, and, and of course now that's happening even more than it used

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:51.380
 to, but, um, and some defiance about it, but, um, but there's a, there's, you know, that

00:45:51.380 --> 00:46:00.080
 drive to, to have, to share food and to share, um, to share with other things with other people

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:08.940
 that is, yeah, I think it's fair to say it's feminist, um, that communal impulse is, um, it's

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:12.620
 just, again, it's a really big part of Iranian culture.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:17.030
 So I do think, I think some men are encouraging it too.

00:46:17.190 --> 00:46:22.580
 Like I know plenty of my family members that are, you know, are going to keep cultivating

00:46:22.580 --> 00:46:27.900
 that feeling, whatever other like logistical requirements have to happen.

00:46:28.040 --> 00:46:38.590
 But, um, I, yeah, I think that I do think that it's, um, it's a requirement in order to

00:46:38.590 --> 00:46:39.230
 be alive.

00:46:41.270 --> 00:46:46.410
 So one other question that I, I, I really wanted to ask you is because you teach writing and

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:50.850
 you teach, cause you teach it, you see Santa Cruz, you teach, and you teach that age, you

00:46:50.850 --> 00:46:56.130
 teach a lot of students who are really are dedicated to, or have the aspiration to make

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:57.750
 a more just world.

00:46:58.310 --> 00:47:05.720
 Um, and you teach writing in a really, with an emphasis on the creative part of writing.

00:47:05.900 --> 00:47:07.420
 So what, what is it?

00:47:07.880 --> 00:47:10.670
 And this is something I've been thinking about in the food movement in general.

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:16.090
 It's like, well, the, the food talking about food, and maybe it's because we talk about

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:21.190
 hunger and really dire situations, but there's this feeling that keeps coming up that it feels

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:23.390
 sometimes oppressively practical.

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:27.500
 So, so what is the role of creativity?

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:31.280
 What is the role of poetics in, in a movement?

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:41.180
 Well, of course, there's always going to be the individuals who are driven by the practical

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:45.920
 and there should be, and the, that that's, that seems the, that seems right.

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:51.590
 And that seems that's somebody's trusting themselves to, and following that.

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:59.440
 And I think the thing about the world now, I mean, the United States in the 21st century

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:04.940
 and what, how, how we're living and how we might, what we might be headed towards is that

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:16.110
 more, um, the people who are, um, um, in inclined to be creative need to be, um, and so not, I

00:48:16.110 --> 00:48:21.750
 don't think everyone is an artist, but a lot of people who could be, aren't because they

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:26.990
 don't, they're, they're, that's not necessarily valued or they didn't learn how to be, they

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:28.510
 had no models for it growing up.

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:38.580
 Um, and so poetics and, um, and creative work of any kind, um, is it's often just a really

00:48:38.580 --> 00:48:42.980
 basic need that an individual has to, to, you know, to find that part of them.

00:48:43.300 --> 00:48:50.790
 Um, I came to understand that I was a creative person or like, you know, could even think of

00:48:50.790 --> 00:48:51.990
 myself that way very late.

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:58.660
 I mean, I was, I was after college that I even thought, oh, that's really, that's important.

00:48:58.660 --> 00:49:01.020
 And I identify with that.

00:49:01.540 --> 00:49:08.630
 Um, and so I'm, you know, I'm, I don't know why it's, I don't, partly, I think because my

00:49:08.630 --> 00:49:13.320
 mother was a, a creative person, I thought I can never be like that.

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:20.240
 You know, I think that was maybe part of it, but, um, but I've not, you know, the, just,

00:49:20.360 --> 00:49:26.090
 I was, I was encouraged to be a good student and, you know, and do good.

00:49:26.090 --> 00:49:31.510
 And, um, I had, and I also was inspired by a lot of teachers in college that were really

00:49:31.510 --> 00:49:36.530
 political and just kind of sort of, you know, also attract, I could, I could think practically

00:49:36.530 --> 00:49:39.030
 and I could, you know, consider, oh, what can I do?

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:41.570
 Um, what should I do in the world?

00:49:41.770 --> 00:49:47.000
 And then, and I think, so, so I think partly I was like experienced that limitation, like

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:51.440
 got, so wasn't encouraged.

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:55.860
 And once I figured out like, well, I'm not going to be, I'm not going to feel like my life

00:49:55.860 --> 00:50:02.590
 is meaningful if I don't make things and part of, and that's part of like that and making,

00:50:02.590 --> 00:50:04.090
 um, important relationships.

00:50:04.090 --> 00:50:05.210
 Those are the key things.

00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:09.530
 I know if I don't have that, my life like feels just not meaningful and I don't want a

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:10.170
 meaningless life.

00:50:10.310 --> 00:50:14.450
 So those are the things, you know, once I figured that out, I'm like, okay, so you

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:20.140
 can't always, you can't have, I was, I was like in my, after grad school, I got this really

00:50:20.140 --> 00:50:24.290
 high paying marketing job and I took it cause I had just coming out of grad school and did

00:50:24.290 --> 00:50:24.690
 it for a year.

00:50:24.710 --> 00:50:25.790
 And I'm like, this sucks.

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:28.290
 I'm not doing anything else in my life at all.

00:50:28.290 --> 00:50:34.030
 And my entire family, you know, they, they, they understand it now, but they're like, you're

00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:35.130
 walking away from that job.

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:36.190
 Like, how can you do that?

00:50:36.290 --> 00:50:37.950
 Cause I can't do it.

00:50:38.410 --> 00:50:45.210
 So, so a very, very physical and visceral experience of blocking that part of my life

00:50:45.210 --> 00:50:45.510
 out.

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:51.730
 I think, I think it means that I don't know, I don't think even half my current students

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:58.370
 necessarily need to be artists or, um, or may, or even want to, but there's a sense of

00:50:58.370 --> 00:51:03.100
 like trying to figure out what makes meaning for each person that is very important.

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:07.610
 And I think we don't talk about that enough in the academic setting.

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:12.750
 And I think there's a way to do that and still, you know, develop professional lives and develop,

00:51:12.890 --> 00:51:16.990
 you know, marketables, you know, perfect careers.

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:20.550
 And, um, so I don't think they have to discount each other.

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:23.200
 And so that, that's the role in the movement.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:28.900
 I think I, you know, I'm not answering that explicitly, but I think we can't say this is

00:51:28.900 --> 00:51:30.520
 how anything should look.

00:51:30.520 --> 00:51:35.960
 Like we can't, we can't say like, it just, it's all about the results and, you know, and

00:51:35.960 --> 00:51:38.420
 there, there, there's just not a place for everyone.

00:51:38.420 --> 00:51:42.380
 If that's what we, if we, everyone has to be practical, everyone has to be like activists.

00:51:42.960 --> 00:51:48.010
 Um, and there needs to be, cause that's, we want, I mean, I think the thing I learned

00:51:48.010 --> 00:51:53.660
 from the political people in that March France and Bettina Aptheker too, that come up that

00:51:53.660 --> 00:51:59.030
 in, when I was in college is it's, it's important to be able to envision the world that

00:51:59.030 --> 00:52:03.970
 we're moving towards and that vision needs to be positive.

00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:06.730
 It needs to be, it needs to be exciting.

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:11.420
 And so, and it's like Emma Goldman's, like, you know, if there, I, if I can't dance, I

00:52:11.420 --> 00:52:12.200
 don't want the revolution.

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:15.140
 I think that's a paraphrase, but that that's important.

00:52:15.140 --> 00:52:20.820
 So, so how do we, I think art, art and art making is so important to that.

00:52:24.140 --> 00:52:26.020
 I'm not telling you anything you don't know.

00:52:27.540 --> 00:52:33.690
 It seems like a great place to end to me.

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:34.410
 Yeah.

00:52:34.530 --> 00:52:35.410
 What a great interview.

00:52:35.530 --> 00:52:35.850
 Is it?

